    "body": "A $4.5 billion expansion at the Bagdad mine in northwestern Arizona would more than double the amount of ore the site can mine and process. Freeport-McMoRan said Nov. 4 that its board approved the project and that it expects the expanded mine to begin producing in mid-2030.\nWhat’s Related\nFreeport expects the project to add about 225 million pounds of copper and 12 million pounds of molybdenum a year. That would bring long-term annual output to more than 400 million pounds of copper and 20 million pounds of molybdenum. Freeport says Bagdad would become the country’s second-largest copper mine, behind its Morenci mine, also in Arizona.\n“We are pleased to proceed with the Bagdad expansion, an important step in advancing Freeport’s U.S. copper growth strategy,” said Kathleen Quirk, Freeport’s president and CEO. The company is moving ahead with engineering, planning equipment purchases, and preparing the site for construction, which it expects to last about three and a half years.\nHow Bagdad will process more ore\nThe biggest addition is a new concentrator that separates valuable minerals from crushed rock. Freeport expects it to add about 115,000 metric tons of daily processing capacity. The project also calls for a new primary crusher, a conveyor to move material across the site, and new water and power lines.\nThe mine will rely on driverless haul trucks to move more material. Freeport completed Bagdad’s fleet conversion in 2025 and says it was the first major U.S. mine to switch entirely to autonomous haulage . During the transition, the company said many former drivers moved into jobs operating the shovels that load the trucks.\nBagdad has been operating since 1945. Freeport says the mine has about 2.6 billion metric tons of ore in its reserves and more than 80 years of reserve life based on current estimates.\nMore than 4,000 new jobs\nFreeport expects to spend about 20% of the project budget in 2027 and another 30% to 35% in 2028, with the rest coming in 2029 and 2030. The company said rising costs could push the final price above its current estimate. It projects more than 4,000 direct and indirect jobs during construction.\nOnce the expansion is running, Freeport expects its long-term cash cost per pound of copper at Bagdad to fall about 20% after accounting for molybdenum sales. The company estimates the project would break even at a copper price of about $4 a pound, assuming molybdenum sells for $20 a pound.",
